     <h2>What is taxed in Spain, and under which rules</h2>
     <p>An heir who is not resident in Spain is taxed here on a territorial basis (obligación real): only on assets and rights located in Spain, or that are exercisable or must be performed here, and on life insurance taken out with Spanish insurers or entered into in Spain with foreign insurers operating here (Law 29/1987, article 7). An heir resident in Spain is taxed on everything inherited, wherever it is (article 6). Residence is decided under the Spanish income tax rules (article 6.2); having an NIE or being registered on a town hall's population register does not decide it by itself.</p>
     <p>The fact that the State manages the tax does not mean the State's rules must be applied. A non-resident heir is entitled to apply the rules of the region where the deceased was resident; and if the deceased was not resident in Spain either, those of the region in which the greatest value of the estate's assets and rights located in Spain is situated (Law 29/1987, second additional provision, paragraph One, letters a and b). According to <a href="https://sede.agenciatributaria.gob.es/Sede/no-residentes/impuesto-sobre-sucesiones-donaciones/preguntas-frecuentes.html" hreflang="es">the Spanish Tax Agency</a>, this option is open to residents of the European Union and the European Economic Area and to residents of other countries alike; the exception is life insurance that does not form part of the estate, which is taxed by the State and only under State rules. Allowances and reliefs differ widely from one region to another, so the State and regional rules should be compared before filing.</p>
+    <p>Two examples show how this works. They illustrate the rules; they are not advice on a particular estate.</p>
+    <p>First example: a mother who was tax resident in Spain dies, and she counts as resident in the Valencian Community because that is where she spent the most days in the five years before her death (Law 29/1987, second additional provision, paragraph One.2). Her son lives in the United Kingdom and is not resident in Spain. Because he is not resident here, the tax is not ceded to the Valencian Community (Law 22/2009, article 32.2): he files form 650 with the Spanish Tax Agency. He may choose the Valencian rules instead of the State rules (Law 29/1987, second additional provision, paragraph One.1.b). The United Kingdom is no longer in the European Union, but that does not remove the choice: according to the Tax Agency, residents of countries outside the Union can make it too.</p>
+    <p>Second example: a father who lived in Germany dies, leaving a flat in Alicante and a bank account in Madrid, and his daughter also lives in Germany. Neither of them is resident in Spain, so she files form 650 with the Tax Agency and is taxed only on what is located in Spain (Law 29/1987, article 7). If she opts for regional rules, they are those of the region where the greatest value of all the estate's assets and rights located in Spain is situated (second additional provision, paragraph One.1.a). The comparison takes in everything the deceased left in Spain, not just the home and not just the part she inherits: if the flat is worth more than the account, the regional rules she may choose are the Valencian ones; if the account is worth more, those of the Community of Madrid.</p>
+    <p>In both cases the regional rules are an option, not an obligation: the tax is worked out under both sets of rules before choosing. The Tax Agency's <a href="https://sede.agenciatributaria.gob.es/Sede/todas-gestiones/impuestos-tasas/impuesto-sobre-sucesiones-donaciones/modelo-650-impue_____iones-autoliquidacion-adquisicion-causa_/cuadro-delimitacion-competencias.html" hreflang="es">table of competences and applicable rules</a> sets out the same distinction between who manages the tax and which rules may be chosen.</p>
     <p>Real estate is valued at its cadastral reference value (valor de referencia) on the date of death; if the value declared is higher, the declared value is used, and if there is no reference value or it cannot be certified, the higher of the declared value and the market value (article 9.3). The reference value can only be challenged by asking for the self-assessment to be corrected or by appealing against an assessment issued by the authorities (article 9.4).</p>
 
